# Tapes and cassettes
Never play a tape you found|It plays you|A man finds a cassette in a secondhand radio; he plays it, and the voice on it describes his room, his chair, and the way he is sitting.|MAN; WIFE; THE VOICE|A drawing room with an old cassette player|He was curious|The voice is the man's, recorded in the future|The cassette, the man's chair, the spinning reel|witness|eerie
Don't rewind a tape that's already at the start|It goes further back|A woman rewinds a tape to the beginning; the tape keeps rewinding, and the room starts getting older around her.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE TAPE|A bedroom with a cassette player|She wanted to hear the first song|The room is returning to the year on the tape|The tape, the peeling wallpaper, the woman's hands|departure|eerie
Never record over a dead person's voice|They record over you|A son records a new message over his dead mother's cassette; his own voice starts disappearing from recordings, then from his throat.|SON; MOTHER'S VOICE; WIFE|A kitchen with a tape recorder|He needed the tape|The mother is recording over him|The recorder, the son's silent mouth, the mother's voice|mimicry|sad
Don't listen to a tape labeled with your name|It's your voice|A woman finds a tape labeled with her name in a box of old things; the voice on it is hers, but it is telling her to do something she hasn't done yet.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE TAPE|An attic with a box of cassettes|She thought it was a childhood recording|The tape is a warning from her future self|The label, the tape, the woman's handwriting|witness|eerie
Never play a tape in a car at night|The back seat joins|A driver plays an old tape on a night road; a third voice joins the recording, and it is coming from the back seat.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE BACK SEAT|A car on a dark highway|He wanted company|The voice is the passenger who died in this car|The back seat, the cassette, the driver's mirror|invitation|eerie
Don't fix a broken cassette with tape|It fixes itself|A boy fixes a broken cassette with cello tape; the tape plays, but the voice on it is his own, asking for help.|BOY; MOTHER; THE CASSETTE|A bedroom with a cassette player|He wanted to hear the song|The cassette is recording him now|The cello tape, the cassette, the boy's mouth|mimicry|eerie
Never give a tape to a child|The child learns the voice|A mother gives her son an old cassette to play with; the boy starts speaking in the voice of a woman who died in the house.|MOTHER; SON; THE VOICE|A child's bedroom with a cassette player|She needed him to be quiet|The voice is the previous owner, teaching him|The cassette, the boy's smile, the mother's hand|mimicry|eerie
Don't play a tape backwards|The message is for you|A man plays a tape backwards for fun; the reversed voice says his name, and the room's lights flicker in time with the syllables.|MAN; WIFE; THE TAPE|A living room with a cassette player|He was bored|The message was recorded backwards on purpose|The tape, the flickering light, the man's name|witness|eerie
Never keep a tape in a hot room|The voice melts|A woman leaves a cassette in a hot car; the tape warps, and the voice starts speaking in a liquid, drowning way.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE TAPE|A car parked in the sun|She forgot it|The voice is the tape's owner, drowning|The warped tape, the hot car, the woman's ear|contamination|eerie
Don't stop a tape mid-sentence|The sentence finishes without you|A man stops a tape mid-sentence; the sentence finishes from the kitchen, in the same voice, and his wife hasn't heard it.|MAN; WIFE; THE VOICE|A living room with a cassette player|He was called away|The tape is finishing the sentence in another room|The stopped tape, the kitchen, the wife's face|mimicry|eerie
# Letters
