Don't buy a radio from a dead person's house|It plays their funeral|A man buys a radio from an estate sale; it only plays one station, and the station is broadcasting the previous owner's funeral.|MAN; WIFE; THE RADIO|A drawing room with a radio|It was vintage|The funeral is still happening|The radio, the funeral, the man's hand|witness|sad
Never turn off a radio mid-song|The song finishes in your head|A woman turns off a radio mid-song; the song continues in her head, and everyone around her can hear it.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE SONG|A kitchen with a radio|She was leaving|The song is now in her voice|The radio, the woman's mouth, the husband's face|mimicry|eerie
Don't tune a radio to a station that doesn't exist|It tunes you|A man tunes his radio to a frequency between stations; the static starts tuning his thoughts.|MAN; WIFE; THE RADIO|A bedroom with a radio|He was curious|The station is broadcasting his thoughts|The radio, the man's blank face, the wife's question|witness|eerie
Never let a child play with a radio|The child repeats the broadcast|A child plays with a radio; he starts repeating a broadcast that hasn't happened yet, including the time of his mother's accident.|MOTHER; CHILD; THE RADIO|A child's bedroom with a radio|He wanted to play|The broadcast is a warning|The radio, the child's drawing, the mother's face|witness|sad
Don't keep a radio in the bedroom|It watches you sleep|A couple keeps a radio in their bedroom; every morning the radio is tuned to a station that describes their dreams.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE RADIO|A bedroom with a radio|They liked music|The radio is recording their sleep|The radio, the sleeping couple, the dial|witness|eerie
# Keys
Never use a key you found|It opens a door you know|A man finds a key on the road; it opens his own front door, but the house inside belongs to someone else.|MAN; WIFE; THE KEY|A doorstep with a found key|He wanted to know what it opened|The house is his, but his family doesn't know him|The key, the open door, the unfamiliar family|threshold|eerie
Don't keep a key that doesn't fit anything|It finds a lock|A woman keeps an old key in her drawer; a new lock appears on a door in her house, and the key fits it.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE KEY|A bedroom with a drawer|She liked the key|The door leads to a room she doesn't remember|The key, the new door, the woman's hand|threshold|eerie
Never give a key to a stranger|They return it with a copy|A man gives a stranger his spare key to hold; the stranger returns it, and a copy is already in the man's lock.|MAN; STRANGER; WIFE|A doorstep at night|The stranger said he was locked out|The stranger has been using the key|The key, the lock, the man's family inside|mimicry|eerie
Don't keep a dead person's keys|They come for them|A son keeps his father's keys; every night he hears keys in the front door, and no one is there.|SON; WIFE; FATHER|A hallway with a key hook|He couldn't throw them away|The father is trying to get in|The keys, the door, the son's hand|invitation|sad
Never open a locked room with a borrowed key|The room borrows you|A tenant opens a locked room with a borrowed key; the room starts borrowing his things, then his memories.|TENANT; LANDLORD; THE ROOM|A rented house with a locked room|He wanted to know what was inside|The room is a copy of his childhood bedroom|The key, the room, the tenant's old toys|threshold|eerie
Don't throw away a key to a demolished house|The house rebuilds|A woman throws away the key to her demolished childhood home; the house starts rebuilding itself around her.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE HOUSE|A street with an empty plot|She wanted to move on|The house is rebuilding from her memory|The key, the new walls, the woman's old room|threshold|sad
Never keep keys in a bowl by the door|Someone counts them|A family keeps keys in a bowl by the door; every morning there is one more key, and one less family member remembers the house.|FAMILY; MOTHER; THE KEYS|An entrance hall with a key bowl|It was convenient|The extra keys are opening doors in other houses|The bowl, the keys, the family photo|counting|eerie
Don't use a key that's warm|Someone just used it|A man picks up a warm key from his table; the door opens before he reaches it, and someone is already inside.|MAN; WIFE; THE VISITOR|A hallway with a key table|He thought it was his|The visitor is the man's future self|The key, the open door, the man's coat|invitation|eerie
Never change the locks on a house that's still occupied|The occupant changes you|A man changes the locks on his inherited house; the house starts changing his memories to match the previous owner's.|MAN; WIFE; THE HOUSE|A house with new locks|He wanted privacy|The previous owner is still living there|The new locks, the old furniture, the man's photo|mimicry|eerie
Don't leave a key in the lock overnight|Someone turns it|A woman leaves her key in the lock overnight; in the morning the door is open, and her key is on the inside.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE VISITOR|A flat with one door|She was tired|The visitor has been living in the spare room|The key, the open door, the spare bed|invitation|eerie
